\section{Drive resistor}
For the 24 V actuator board, let \(V_d\) be the voltage drop across resistor D17, \(I_d\) the current entering its marked positive terminal, and \(R_d\) its resistance. The passive sign convention is used for this component. The accepted relation is
\[V_d = I_d R_d.\]

\section{Sense resistor}
In the isolated current monitor, \(R_d\) is reused locally for the resistance of shunt S2; it is not D17. With \(V_s\) and \(I_s\) referenced by the passive sign convention, the shunt relation is
\[V_s = I_s R_d.\]

\section{Method}
Run C-14 concerns one sealed copper coupon of mass \(m\), with constant specific heat \(c\) over its measured temperature rise \(\Delta T\); no phase change occurred. The method sheet states that the sensible heat received by that coupon is
\[Q = m c \Delta T.\]

\section{Release calculation}
This release note explicitly uses the same run C-14, the same coupon, and the same heat-in convention as the method sheet. Despite those shared references, it certifies the incompatible claim
\[Q = 2 m c \Delta T.\]

\section{Static column check}
The water column was at rest and treated as constant density \(\rho\). Height \(h\) is measured downward from the free surface, the gauge pressure there is zero, and \(g\) is the positive magnitude of gravitational acceleration. The gauge transducer therefore follows
\[p = \rho g h.\]

\section{Run 41}
At the particle location the probe logged an electric field \(\mathbf E\) and a magnetic field \(\mathbf B\). The next sentence says only that "this field" is denoted by \(\mathbf X\), without identifying which of the two logged fields it means. Magnetic-force terms are declared outside the intended model, and the notebook writes
\[\mathbf F = q\mathbf X.\]

\section{Reconstruction operators}
The reconstruction uses two distinct matrices: the calibrated forward operator \(A\in\mathbb R^{m\times n}\) and a preconditioner \(B\in\mathbb R^{m\times q}\), where \(q\ne n\). A vector \(x\in\mathbb R^n\) and output \(y\in\mathbb R^m\) are then introduced. The phrase "that matrix" is not resolved to either preceding matrix, yet the draft assigns it the symbol \(M\) and states
\[y = Mx.\]

\section{Conditional failure rate}
Events \(A\) (seal leakage) and \(B\) (over-temperature operation) belong to the same mission probability space. The latter event, explicitly meaning \(B\), has positive probability. Conditioning leakage on that latter event yields
\[P(A\mid B)=\frac{P(A\cap B)}{P(B)}.\]

\section{Resonance metric}
This acoustics report defines \(Q\) solely as the dimensionless quality factor of a duct resonance, \(f_0\) as its center frequency, and \(b\) as its bandwidth. Neither cross-sectional area nor mean normal velocity is represented by these symbols. The reported metric is
\[Q = \frac{f_0}{b}.\]

\section{Pixel statistic}
In this data specification, \(P\) denotes a dimensionless normalized pixel score, \(V\) is the number of valid frames, and \(I\) is the summed pixel intensity in digital counts. They are not electrical power, voltage, or current, and no electrical sign convention applies. The normalization rule is
\[P = V I.\]

\section{Shared notation}
A thermal paragraph defines \(T\) as absolute temperature, while an adjacent vibration paragraph also defines \(T\) as the measured positive period of a fan tone. The symbol \(f\) is the fan-tone frequency. The draft gives no scope marker indicating which meaning of \(T\) is active in the following line:
\[f = \frac{1}{T}.\]

\section{Harness H3}
For resistor R7, voltage \(V\) is positive at the terminal where current \(I\) enters; \(R>0\) is its resistance. The draft states
\[V = I R.\]

\section{Cell G9}
The sealed cell is modeled as an ideal gas at absolute temperature \(T\), with pressure \(p\), volume \(V\), amount \(n\), and gas constant \(R\). For state S9 the submitted model states
\[pV = nRT.\]

\section{Reviewed heuristic}
The reviewer removes the gradient model and replaces it with a controller-specific correction. Here \(r_k\) is merely a signed sensor residual; it is explicitly not a gradient of an objective, and \(\eta\) is its empirical gain. The accepted heuristic is
\[\theta_{k+1}=\theta_k+\eta r_k.\]
